tom edge of the paper  and again just make a crease and use  your fingernail so when you open it  you'll see you have an X and a  horizontal line in the middle what you  want to do now is to take the top and  bottom edges and fold it on to this  midline that we now have so take the top  edge and fold it down to that midline  and make a crease and do the same with  the bottom edge  so when you unfold it you'll now see  that we have four horizontal sections  we're going to do the same going this  way now so just rotate the paper so that  these lines are vertical and then you  could do the same by taking the top edge  folding the paper down in half and make  a crease  open it up and then once again fold the  top and bottom edges onto the midline  so once you unfold now you will notice  that you have an X in the paper and you  also have 16 squares in the paper as  well once you have a piece of paper that  looks like this we're going to take the  bottom half of the paper and fold it up  to the top half and make sure everything  is nice and flat for the next fold I'd  like you to just visualize the distance  between the bottom end here and this  first horizontal fold this distance here  now if you split that distance into  thirds we're going to fold up the very  bottom third of the paper so to help you  visualize this I have drawn on another  model now by all means you do not have  to draw on a model to understand this  however I've just done this to make it  easier for you to see we're going to  fold up the very bottom third of that  distance so you're going to fold it up  just like that and as you'll see the  amount that we have folded up this space  here is the same space same distance as  the distance from the edge to that fold  there so we're going to fold the bottom  edge up until the distance that we have  folded up is equal to the distance that  we have remaining to that first  horizontal fold just like that so as  you'll see it's about the same this  distance is 